        <body>
            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="1076" n="926" sameAs="frus:frus1907p2/pg926"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 6924/3–4.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Peirce to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Christiania</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-11-06"><hi rendition="#italic">November 6,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 85.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith a copy
                    and translation of a note, just received, from the minister of foreign affairs,
                    informing me that the Governments of Norway, France, and Great Britain have, on
                    November 2, signed a declaration concerning the abrogation, as regards Norway,
                    of the treaty of November 21, 1855, between the United Kingdoms of Norway and
                    Sweden and France and Great Britain, guaranteeing the integrity of the
                    Scandinavian Peninsula, and that immediately thereafter a treaty concerning the
                    integrity of Norway was signed by Mr. Lövland, on the part of this Government,
                    together with the diplomatic representatives of Germany, France, Great Britain,
                    and Russia, who were duly authorized by their respective Governments to that
                    end.</p>
                <p>The minister informs me further that, Norway having made the reservation that the
                    treaty shall be submitted to the Storting before ratification, the text of
                    neither the declaration of abrogation nor of the treaty shall be published until
                    after ratification.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herbert H. D.
                    Peirce.</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d301-a1.xml" xpointer="d301-a1"/>
            </div>
